1. Forge (Card-Forge/forge)
---------------------------

mtg-table uses Forge as its rules engine and AI opponent.

    Project:  Card-Forge/forge
    Source:   https://github.com/Card-Forge/forge
    Licence:  GNU General Public License v3.0 or later (GPL-3.0-or-later)
    Version:  2.0.14 (installed locally by the user; jar manifest reads
              "2.0.14-SNAPSHOT-08.08")

How it is used:

  * Forge is used UNMODIFIED. mtg-table contains no Forge source code and applies
    no patches to Forge.
  * Forge is a compile-time and runtime dependency only. The bridge in bridge/
    compiles against, and runs on, a Forge jar that is already installed on the
    user's machine (default:
    /home/justin-a/forge/forge-gui-desktop-2.0.14-jar-with-dependencies.jar).
    The jar path is read from config.json or the FORGE_JAR environment variable.
  * This repository NEVER redistributes Forge. The Forge jar, Forge's res/ tree,
    Forge's cardsfolder card scripts, Forge's editions files and Forge's skin art
    are not committed here and are not shipped in any build output. *.jar is
    gitignored; forge-home/res is a symlink into the user's own Forge install and
    is gitignored.
  * mtg-table's Java bridge implements Forge interfaces (forge.gui.interfaces.IGuiBase,
    forge.gamemodes.match.AbstractGuiGame) on the same classpath, in the same JVM.
    That makes mtg-table a derivative work of Forge, which is why the whole of this
    repository is GPL-3.0-or-later.

Forge bundles its own third-party dependencies inside that jar (Netty, Jetty,
Guava, XStream, JGraphT, tinylog, Sentry and others), each under its own licence.
Those licences are carried by the Forge distribution the user installed; mtg-table
neither redistributes nor relicenses them.

Forge is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast. Neither is mtg-table.


2. Scryfall (card data and card images)
---------------------------------------

    Service:  Scryfall
    Site:     https://scryfall.com
    API docs: https://scryfall.com/docs/api
    Terms:    https://scryfall.com/docs/api  (see also /docs/api/rate-limits)

mtg-table resolves card art through the Scryfall API (tools/fetch-art.ts) and
displays card images served from cards.scryfall.io. Card data and images from
Scryfall are used subject to Scryfall's API terms, which mtg-table honours as
follows:

  * User-Agent: every request to api.scryfall.com sends an accurate, identifying
    User-Agent (configured as "scryfallUserAgent" in config.json) and an explicit
    Accept: application/json header. HTTP libraries are never left to choose these
    headers.
  * Rate limits: requests to /cards/search, /cards/named, /cards/random and
    /cards/collection are throttled to at most 2 per second (>= 500 ms apart);
    all other API methods to at most 10 per second. HTTP 429 responses are backed
    off, honouring Retry-After. Image file origins under *.scryfall.io are not
    rate-limited but are still fetched sparingly.
  * Caching: resolved art is written once to web/public/art.json and reused, so a
    session makes no repeat API calls. Bulk endpoints are used for anything at
    scale.
  * Artist credit: wherever card art is shown, the artist name and the card's
    copyright line are shown in the same interface (card-detail panel). Card
    images are not distorted, skewed, stretched, blurred, sharpened, desaturated,
    colour-shifted, watermarked or overlaid with logos, and the printed copyright
    and artist credit on a card image are never cropped or covered.
  * No paywalling, no implication of Scryfall endorsement, and no bare
    repackaging or proxying of Scryfall data.

Scryfall is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast, and its own footer notes
that portions of Scryfall are unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Wizards
of the Coast Fan Content Policy.


