5. mana-font (mana symbols)
---------------------------

    Package:  mana-font 1.18.0 (npm), https://github.com/andrewgioia/mana
    Code:     MIT License (the CSS and the build)
    Font:     SIL Open Font License 1.1 (the glyphs in fonts/mana.*)
    Symbols:  the mana symbols themselves are copyright Wizards of the Coast

Added in M4 (docs/research/10-board-ui-spec.md §13). It is the web client's only
runtime style dependency: `web/src/main.tsx` imports `mana-font/css/mana.css` and
`web/src/styles.css` overrides its `@font-face` to load the woff2 the package
ships (187 KB) instead of the woff its own CSS lists (408 KB). Vite emits the
font files into `web/dist/assets/`; nothing is hot-linked and no font file is
committed to this repository.

MIT and OFL-1.1 are both GPL-3.0-compatible, so bundling it changes nothing about
this repository's licence. The symbols it draws are Wizards of the Coast's, and
their use here falls under the Fan Content Policy recorded in section 3 — the
same basis as the card images in section 2.

mana-font is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast. Neither is mtg-table.


6. three.js, react-three-fiber, react-three-drei (the M7 arena)
---------------------------------------------------------------

    Package:  three 0.186.0 (npm), https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js
    Licence:  MIT — its LICENSE reads "The MIT License / Copyright (c) 2010-2026
              three.js authors"

    Package:  @react-three/fiber 8.18.0 (npm),
              https://github.com/pmndrs/react-three-fiber
    Licence:  MIT — declared in package.json; the published tarball carries no
              LICENSE file of its own

    Package:  @react-three/drei 9.122.0 (npm), https://github.com/pmndrs/drei
    Licence:  MIT — its LICENSE reads "Copyright (c) 2020 react-spring"

    Package:  @types/three 0.185.4 (npm, devDependency only — three ships no
              types of its own), https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped
    Licence:  MIT (DefinitelyTyped; "Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation")

Added in M7 (docs/research/09-arena-tier1-plan.md §1). They are the arena's
renderer: the 3D presentation of the same frame stream the 2D board renders.
Versions are pinned EXACTLY, with no caret, for a stated reason — fiber 8 and
drei 9 are the React-18 line, frozen since 2025-02-19 with no maintenance
branch, and fiber 8's `three` peer range (">=0.133") has no upper bound, so a
floating range would eventually install a `three` that the frozen line cannot
be fixed for (report 09 §1.2, §13).

They are bundled into the browser client and lazily code-split: nothing is
hot-linked, and the 2D board's entry chunk does not carry them. Vite emits the
minified code into web/dist/assets/; their MIT copyright notices are carried in
the license comments the bundler preserves.

MIT is GPL-3.0-compatible in the direction that matters here (MIT code may be
combined into a GPL-3.0-or-later work), so bundling them changes nothing about
this repository's licence and adds no AGPL exposure. None of them is affiliated
with Wizards of the Coast, and none ships any Magic content: the card images the
arena puts on its quads are the same Scryfall images as section 2, fetched at
runtime and never committed. The card back the arena draws is generated
procedurally in code (web/src/arena/scene/textFrame.ts) rather than scanned,
because the printed Magic card back is trademarked artwork.


